# A fixed defect, named after it (CLAUDE.md §9; Go's `test/fixedbugs`).
#
# SYMPTOM, 2026-09-02: this program compiled at exit 0 and printed `7` then
# `101`. `state.value` is written twice and meant two different things — the
# local record's FIELD in `main`, the module's FUNCTION in `elsewhere` — with
# no diagnostic anywhere. Both readings type-check, because a field of `i64`
# and a function returning `i64` are indistinguishable at the use site.
#
# CAUSE: `selfhost/resolve/state.hero`'s `declare` ran three name-is-taken
# stages — another local, a built-in, a top-level declaration — and never
# consulted the file's `use` table. The declaration direction was refused
# (`use_shadows_a_declaration`); the local direction was not. spec:99 plus
# `use` BINDS already forbade it: panel 031 R3 chose that verb for exactly
# this coverage, at `DESIGN-LOG:185`, and the compiler never delivered it.
# So CLAUDE.md §12 — the spec was right and the compiler had the bug.
#
# FIX: a fourth stage, first of the three, because a `use` line is in this
# file and always openable. Found by M-selfhost-nesting, whose whole job was
# to drop the prefixes that had been hiding the class: nobody names a local
# `check_state`, everybody names one `state`.
use state

record Box
    value: i64

function elsewhere() -> i64
    return state.value(1)

function main()
    state = Box(value: 7)  #~ shadowed_binding
    print(to_str(state.value))
    print(to_str(elsewhere()))

# Since 2026-09-10 the specification's sections are numbered and a citation names
# the section, never a line (CL-037). The line numbers above were written against
# the layout at commit 834d804f; read today they are:
#   spec:99 is spec § 5 Bindings plus
